  <h2>1. Introduction</h2>
  <p>This report documents the work completed for Assignment 2: API Server Checklist for the Fragments microservice. It builds on the code delivered in Assignment 1 and adds support for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>POST handling for text/* and application/json payloads</li>
    <li>GET /v1/fragments?expand=1 returning expanded metadata</li>
    <li>GET /v1/fragments/:id/info returning fragment metadata</li>
    <li>GET /v1/fragments/:id.ext supporting markdown (.md) → HTML (.html) via markdown-it</li>
    <li>Multi-stage Dockerfile and CI pipeline that builds and pushes Docker images</li>
    <li>Unit tests and coverage for the above functionality</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Repository and CI links</h2>
  <p>Private GitHub repository (source): [PRIVATE REPO URL - replace with your private repo link]</p>
  <p>Public Docker Hub repository (images): https://hub.docker.com/repository/docker/harshpahurkar/fragments</p>
  <p>GitHub Actions CI (example run): [link to successful ci run — copy from Actions tab]</p>

  <h2>3. Implementation summary</h2>
  <p>Key files added/modified:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>src/routes/api/post.js</code> — Accepts application/json and text/*, normalizes JSON before storage.</li>
    <li><code>src/routes/api/get.js</code> — Supports <code>?expand=1</code> by forwarding to the model.</li>
    <li><code>src/routes/api/getById.js</code> — Returns content with appropriate Content-Type. If stored JSON is corrupted, returns raw content as <code>text/plain</code>.</li>
    <li><code>src/routes/api/getInfo.js</code> — Returns metadata for a fragment.</li>
    <li><code>src/routes/api/getByIdExt.js</code> — Supports <code>id.html</code> converting markdown to HTML via <code>markdown-it</code>.</li>
    <li><code>src/model/fragments.js</code> — Added expand behavior and exported <code>getFragmentMeta</code>.</li>
    <li><code>Dockerfile</code> — Multi-stage Dockerfile producing a small runtime image.</li>
    <li><code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code> — CI pipeline: lint, unit-tests+coverage, build & push image to Docker Hub.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Evidence & Screenshots (with exact reproduction steps)</h2>
  <p>Below are the required screenshots and exact steps/commands so a grader can reproduce each screenshot from scratch. Where I could not produce live screenshots (UI/Cognito), I included placeholders and explicit steps to capture them.</p>

  <h3>Figure 4.1 — Docker Hub tags page</h3>
  <div class="figure">
    <div class="caption">What to capture: Docker Hub repository page showing tags: <code>latest</code>, <code>main</code>, and commit SHA tags.</div>
    <p>How to reproduce:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open https://hub.docker.com and log in to your Docker Hub account.</li>
      <li>Navigate to <code>harshpahurkar/fragments</code> (or your Docker Hub repo name).</li>
      <li>Open the Tags view and take a screenshot showing the listed tags and timestamps.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h3>Figure 4.2 — GitHub Actions CI run showing successful docker push</h3>
  <div class="figure">
    <div class="caption">What to capture: GitHub Actions run page for the <code>ci</code> workflow. Expand the job labeled "Build and push" (docker/build-push-action) and screenshot the logs showing the push lines for the tags.</div>
    <p>How to reproduce:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to your GitHub repo → Actions → click the most recent <code>ci</code> run (green).</li>
      <li>Click the job that includes the docker push step (it may be called "docker-hub" or "Build and push").</li>
      <li>Expand the <code>Build and push</code> step and screenshot the output that shows lines like "pushing" or "pushed" and repository digests.</li>
